{"database":{"advisory-count":1458,"last-commit":null,"last-updated":null},"lockfile":{"dependency-count":449},"settings":{"target_arch":[],"target_os":[],"severity":null,"ignore":[],"informational_warnings":["unmaintained","unsound","notice"]},"vulnerabilities":{"found":true,"count":6,"list":[{"advisory":{"id":"RUSTSEC-2026-0195","package":"quick-xml","title":"Unbounded namespace-declaration allocation in `NsReader` enables memory-exhaustion denial of service","description":"`NsReader` resolves namespaces by calling `NamespaceResolver::push` for every\n`Start`/`Empty` event *before* the event is returned to the caller. `push`\niterated all `xmlns` / `xmlns:*` attributes on the start tag and, for each one,\nappended the prefix bytes to an internal buffer and pushed a `NamespaceBinding`\n(32 bytes on 64-bit) to an internal `Vec`, with no upper bound on the number of\ndeclarations.\n\n## Impact\n\nA start tag with `N` namespace declarations drove roughly `3×` the tag's byte\nsize in `NamespaceResolver` heap, allocated *inside* `quick-xml` before the\n`NsReader` consumer ever received the event and could inspect or reject it. A\nconsumer that bounds its *input* size therefore still cannot bound this\nallocation: an `M`-byte start tag yields on the order of `3 × M` bytes of\nresolver heap the caller never sees.\n\nOn untrusted XML this lets a remote, unauthenticated attacker force large heap\nallocations with a single start tag. With several `NsReader`s running\nconcurrently on independent inputs (a common server pattern), the allocations\nstack and can exhaust process memory, causing the operating system to kill the\nprocess (OOM). This was confirmed against a real-world RPKI relying party (NLnet\nLabs Routinator), where concurrent RRDP validation workers parsing a crafted\n`snapshot.xml` exceeded the memory limit and the process was OOM-killed.\n\n## Affected code paths\n\nConsumers using `NsReader` (which always calls `NamespaceResolver::push` before\nyielding `Start`/`Empty`), or calling `NamespaceResolver::push` directly. A plain\n`Reader` that does not perform namespace resolution is not affected.\n\n## Remediation\n\nUpgrade to `quick-xml >= 0.41.0`. `NamespaceResolver::push` now rejects a start\ntag that declares more than `DEFAULT_MAX_DECLARATIONS_PER_ELEMENT` (256)\nnamespace bindings, returning the new `NamespaceError::TooManyDeclarations`\ninstead of allocating without limit. The limit is configurable via\n`NamespaceResolver::set_max_declarations_per_element` (use `usize::MAX` to\nrestore the previous unbounded behavior), and `NsReader::resolver_mut()` is\nprovided to reach it.\n\nThere is no clean workaround for `NsReader` consumers before 0.41.0, as the\nallocation happens inside the reader with no configuration knob to cap it.","date":"2026-06-29","aliases":[],"related":[],"collection":"crates","categories":["denial-of-service"],"keywords":["xml","parser","dos","memory","namespace"],"cvss":"CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H","informational":null,"references":["https://github.com/tafia/quick-xml/commit/7ca25266e94987210daa864889ab15c9332c8a2a"],"source":null,"url":"https://github.com/tafia/quick-xml/issues/970","withdrawn":null,"license":"CC0-1.0","expect-deleted":false},"versions":{"patched":[">=0.41.0"],"unaffected":[]},"affected":null,"package":{"name":"quick-xml","version":"0.39.4","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index","checksum":"cdcc8dd4e2f670d309a5f0e83fe36dfdc05af317008fea29144da1a2ac858e5e","dependencies":[{"name":"memchr","version":"2.8.3","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"serde","version":"1.0.229","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"}],"replace":null}},{"advisory":{"id":"RUSTSEC-2026-0194","package":"quick-xml","title":"Quadratic run time when checking a start tag for duplicate attribute names","description":"`BytesStart::attributes()` returns an `Attributes` iterator which, by default\n(`with_checks(true)`), rejects a start tag that repeats an attribute name. For\neach attribute yielded, the iterator compared the new name against every name\nseen so far in the same tag using a linear scan, so a start tag with `N`\ndistinct attribute names cost `O(N²)` byte comparisons. There was no bound on\n`N` other than the size of the buffered start tag.\n\n## Impact\n\nAny code that parses untrusted XML and iterates a start tag's attributes with\nthe default duplicate check enabled can be made to spend CPU time quadratic in\nthe number of attributes on a single tag. Because the check is pure computation\nwith no `.await`/I/O, an I/O-based timeout on the consumer (for example a read\nor request timeout) cannot interrupt it while it runs.\n\nMeasured cost of a single start tag, release build:\n\n| Attributes on one tag | Time |\n|---|---|\n| 80,000  | ~6 s   |\n| 800,000 | ~10 min |\n\nThe cost grows with the square of the attribute count, so a start tag of a few\ntens of megabytes can stall a parsing thread for hours. No memory is exhausted\nand the parser does not crash; the effect is CPU exhaustion on the thread doing\nthe parsing: a single crafted start tag can pin a CPU core for minutes to hours,\ndenying service to that worker. A deployment that places a wall-clock bound on\nparsing, or confines it to a non-critical thread, may consider the availability\nimpact lower.\n\n## Affected code paths\n\n* `BytesStart::attributes()` / `Attributes` iterated with checks enabled (the\n  default), and `BytesStart::try_get_attribute`.\n* `NsReader`, which resolves namespaces by iterating a tag's attributes and so\n  reaches the same check internally.\n\nConsumers that iterate attributes with `.attributes().with_checks(false)` and do\nnot use `NsReader` are not affected.\n\nThis was reported as reachable by a remote, unauthenticated attacker in a\nreal-world RPKI relying party (NLnet Labs Routinator) via a crafted RRDP\n`snapshot.xml`.\n\n## Remediation\n\nUpgrade to `quick-xml >= 0.41.0`, where the duplicate check keeps the linear\nscan for start tags with a small number of attributes and switches to an `O(1)`\nhash pre-filter above a threshold, making the whole tag `O(N)`. The reported\n`AttrError::Duplicated` positions are unchanged.\n\nIf upgrading is not possible and duplicate-name detection is not required,\ndisable it with `.attributes().with_checks(false)` (this does not help\n`NsReader` consumers, which have no equivalent opt-out before 0.41.0).","date":"2026-06-29","aliases":[],"related":[],"collection":"crates","categories":["denial-of-service"],"keywords":["xml","parser","dos","algorithmic-complexity","quadratic"],"cvss":"CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H","informational":null,"references":["https://github.com/tafia/quick-xml/pull/971","https://github.com/tafia/quick-xml/commit/07f3db8343cf152f5bc3483ef5b3164582489bea"],"source":null,"url":"https://github.com/tafia/quick-xml/issues/969","withdrawn":null,"license":"CC0-1.0","expect-deleted":false},"versions":{"patched":[">=0.41.0"],"unaffected":[]},"affected":null,"package":{"name":"quick-xml","version":"0.39.4","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index","checksum":"cdcc8dd4e2f670d309a5f0e83fe36dfdc05af317008fea29144da1a2ac858e5e","dependencies":[{"name":"memchr","version":"2.8.3","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"serde","version":"1.0.229","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"}],"replace":null}},{"advisory":{"id":"RUSTSEC-2026-0391","package":"zstd","title":"Data from an abandoned stream leaks into the next stream on a reused context","description":"The `with_context` constructors of the stream encoders and decoders used the\nborrowed context as it was. When a stream was abandoned on a context, for\nexample an encoder dropped before `finish()` after an error, whatever it\nleft there was carried into the next stream: the input buffered by the\nprevious encoder (up to 128 KiB, or all of it with `zstdmt`) became the\nstart of the next frame, and a decoder returned undelivered output of the\nprevious stream.\n\nAn application reusing one context for data that must stay separate, such\nas different users' requests, could disclose one user's plaintext to\nanother. Whoever supplies the data can cause the abandonment with invalid\ninput.\n\nThe flaw was corrected by resetting the context's session in\n`with_context`.","date":"2026-10-16","aliases":[],"related":[],"collection":"crates","categories":["memory-exposure"],"keywords":["information-leak","context-reuse"],"cvss":null,"informational":null,"references":["https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/pull/384"],"source":null,"url":"https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/commit/74ace2dcdca62a2ce0f034895d6079458311e003","withdrawn":null,"license":"CC0-1.0","expect-deleted":false},"versions":{"patched":["^0.13.4",">=0.14.3"],"unaffected":["<0.13.2"]},"affected":{"arch":[],"os":[],"functions":{"zstd::stream::raw::Decoder::with_context":[">=0.13.2, <0.13.4",">=0.14.0, <0.14.3"],"zstd::stream::raw::Encoder::with_context":[">=0.13.2, <0.13.4",">=0.14.0, <0.14.3"]}},"package":{"name":"zstd","version":"0.13.3","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index","checksum":"e91ee311a569c327171651566e07972200e76fcfe2242a4fa446149a3881c08a","dependencies":[{"name":"zstd-safe","version":"7.3.0","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"}],"replace":null}},{"advisory":{"id":"RUSTSEC-2026-0403","package":"zstd-safe","title":"Uninitialized memory exposed when streaming decompression of a legacy frame fails","description":"When decompression fails inside a legacy (zstd v0.4 to v0.7) frame, the\nbundled zstd library reports the output buffer as fully written although\nnothing was written. `DCtx::decompress_stream` committed that position even\non error, and for a `Vec<u8>` destination this extended the vector's length\nover uninitialized memory.\n\nLegacy support is enabled by default. A 5- or 6-byte corrupt legacy frame is\nenough: through `zstd::stream::write::Decoder`, up to 32 KiB of stale heap\nmemory is then passed to the wrapped writer on the next `write()` or\n`flush()`, or when an `auto_flush()`/`on_flush()` decoder is dropped. Whether\nthis reaches an attacker depends on where the application sends the\ndecompressed output. `zstd::stream::read::Decoder`, `zstd::decode_all` and\nthe bulk API are not affected.\n\nBefore zstd-safe 4.0.0, output buffers could only be initialized slices.\nThe zstd crate's own writer had the same flaw from 0.4.22 to 0.7.x, which is\ncovered by a separate advisory.\n\nThe flaw was corrected by only committing the buffer positions of a\nsuccessful call.","date":"2026-10-16","aliases":[],"related":[],"collection":"crates","categories":["memory-exposure"],"keywords":["uninitialized-memory","decompression","legacy"],"cvss":null,"informational":null,"references":["https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/pull/384"],"source":null,"url":"https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/commit/006e1d1ad1eeaf6b5d4c28329e70217adabcec43","withdrawn":null,"license":"CC0-1.0","expect-deleted":false},"versions":{"patched":["^7.4.0",">=8.2.0"],"unaffected":["<4.0.0"]},"affected":{"arch":[],"os":[],"functions":{"zstd_safe::DCtx::decompress_stream":[">=4.0.0, <7.4.0",">=8.0.0, <8.2.0"]}},"package":{"name":"zstd-safe","version":"7.3.0","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index","checksum":"64d80649ab6db9d9f6f9c80a40becd948eda4714a0a5ac8c4d157a32231c7882","dependencies":[{"name":"zstd-sys","version":"2.1.1+zstd.1.5.7","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"}],"replace":null}},{"advisory":{"id":"RUSTSEC-2026-0398","package":"zstd-safe","title":"`Seekable::decompress` sets a `Vec` length beyond its capacity for offsets past the end","description":"`ZSTD_seekable_decompress` shortens the requested length to the end of the\ndata without checking that the offset is before it. For an offset past the\nend, it returns a wrapped-around length without writing anything, and\n`Seekable::decompress` passed that length to `Vec::set_len`.\n\nWith the `seekable` feature, whoever supplies the archive, or chooses the\noffset, can leave the caller's `Vec<u8>` with a length far beyond its\ncapacity. Reading it then discloses neighbouring heap memory, and appending\nto it corrupts memory. Offsets near `u64::MAX` can also hang the call.\n\nThe flaw was corrected by clamping requests to the end of the data, and by\nchecking every count reported by the C library against the buffer capacity.","date":"2026-10-16","aliases":[],"related":[],"collection":"crates","categories":["memory-exposure","memory-corruption"],"keywords":["seekable","out-of-bounds"],"cvss":null,"informational":null,"references":["https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/pull/384"],"source":null,"url":"https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/commit/80506be743fae2ed0c2fe8b21c374c6a2eab0be7","withdrawn":null,"license":"CC0-1.0","expect-deleted":false},"versions":{"patched":["^7.4.0",">=8.2.0"],"unaffected":["<7.2.2"]},"affected":{"arch":[],"os":[],"functions":{"zstd_safe::seekable::AdvancedSeekable::decompress":[">=7.2.2, <7.4.0",">=8.0.0, <8.2.0"],"zstd_safe::seekable::Seekable::decompress":[">=7.2.2, <7.4.0",">=8.0.0, <8.2.0"]}},"package":{"name":"zstd-safe","version":"7.3.0","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index","checksum":"64d80649ab6db9d9f6f9c80a40becd948eda4714a0a5ac8c4d157a32231c7882","dependencies":[{"name":"zstd-sys","version":"2.1.1+zstd.1.5.7","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"}],"replace":null}},{"advisory":{"id":"RUSTSEC-2026-0410","package":"zstd-sys","title":"WebAssembly allocator shim corrupts memory on allocation failure","description":"On wasm32, zstd-sys provides `malloc` and `free` to the C library through a\nshim over Rust's allocator. The shim did not check for allocation failure:\nit wrote its size header to address 0 and returned address 4, which the C\nlibrary used as a valid buffer. `free(NULL)` also read below address 0.\n\nWhenever the instance cannot grow its memory by what a frame asks for, a\nsmall crafted frame (a 9-byte header declaring a 128 MiB window is enough)\nmade decompression write decoded data over the bottom of the module's\nlinear memory, instead of failing. The corruption stays within the wasm\nsandbox.\n\nThe flaw was corrected by returning NULL on allocation failure or size\noverflow, and by making `free(NULL)` a no-op.","date":"2026-10-16","aliases":[],"related":[],"collection":"crates","categories":["memory-corruption"],"keywords":["wasm","allocation"],"cvss":null,"informational":null,"references":["https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/pull/384"],"source":null,"url":"https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/commit/165d1625553c236b9700ca794b8a7c51f5d98314","withdrawn":null,"license":"CC0-1.0","expect-deleted":false},"versions":{"patched":[">=2.1.2"],"unaffected":["<2.0.9"]},"affected":{"arch":["wasm32"],"os":[],"functions":{}},"package":{"name":"zstd-sys","version":"2.1.1+zstd.1.5.7","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index","checksum":"aeec9eaf2dffbbd09201e23bd0ffcbaa33bb8e9266a10734fd7ed90a85eca078","dependencies":[{"name":"cc","version":"1.8.0","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"pkg-config","version":"0.3.34","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"}],"replace":null}}]},"warnings":{"unsound":[{"kind":"unsound","package":{"name":"arrow-data","version":"59.3.1","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index","checksum":"54f128c73bb7fe9f7137218f910edd5b436f390e0f040b1956f5028390787897","dependencies":[{"name":"arrow-buffer","version":"59.3.1","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"arrow-schema","version":"59.3.1","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"half","version":"2.7.1","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"num-integer","version":"0.1.47","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"num-traits","version":"0.2.19","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"}],"replace":null},"advisory":{"id":"RUSTSEC-2026-0442","package":"arrow-data","title":"Apache Arrow Rust: Improper validation of RunEndEncoded arrays allows out of bounds reads","description":"Improper validation of RunEndEncoded arrays via `ArrayData::validate_values` can\nallow reading data beyond the bounds of the child values array when reading the\nlast value via APIs such as `TypedRunArray::value`.\n\nPatched versions now properly validate the contents of RunEndEncoded arrays to prevent\nallowing reading out of bounds of the specified array data.\n\nCredit to Adam Korczynski of Ada Logics for reporting this and Anthropic for finding\nthis.","date":"2026-11-02","aliases":["CVE-2026-111959","GHSA-gqmm-qrjq-4c7h"],"related":[],"collection":"crates","categories":["memory-exposure"],"keywords":["out-of-bounds-read","validation"],"cvss":null,"informational":"unsound","references":["https://www.openwall.com/lists/oss-security/2026/11/02/2"],"source":null,"url":"https://github.com/apache/arrow-rs/issues/11587","withdrawn":null,"license":"CC0-1.0","expect-deleted":false},"affected":null,"versions":{"patched":[">=60.1.0",">=59.3.2, <60.0.0",">=58.4.1, <59.0.0",">=57.3.2, <58.0.0"],"unaffected":["<33.0.0"]}},{"kind":"unsound","package":{"name":"rustix","version":"1.1.5","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index","checksum":"891efababe418670775f199f0d233d84843c227a0949a883ce15b37c78d6629d","dependencies":[{"name":"bitflags","version":"2.13.2","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"errno","version":"0.3.14","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"libc","version":"0.2.190","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"linux-raw-sys","version":"0.12.1","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"windows-sys","version":"0.61.2","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"}],"replace":null},"advisory":{"id":"RUSTSEC-2026-0486","package":"rustix","title":"xattr functions mark unwritten bytes as initialized when given a zero-length buffer","description":"The extended-attribute functions in `rustix::fs` (`getxattr`, `lgetxattr`, `fgetxattr`, `listxattr`, `llistxattr` and `flistxattr`) write into a `Buffer` and then mark as many bytes initialized as the system call reported. When the buffer has zero length, the operating system treats the call as a size query: it writes nothing and returns the size of the attribute value or of the name list. rustix still marked that many bytes as initialized.\n\nPassing a `Vec` whose length equals its capacity through `spare_capacity` gives such a zero-length buffer, and rustix then set the vector's length past its capacity over memory that was never written. This is undefined behavior reachable from safe code. With an empty `&mut [MaybeUninit<u8>]` the call panics instead.\n\nFrom 1.1.6 these functions return an empty result for a zero-length buffer, and new `getxattr_query`, `lgetxattr_query`, `fgetxattr_query`, `listxattr_query`, `llistxattr_query` and `flistxattr_query` functions return the size. The 0.38 series did not have the `Buffer` API and is not affected.","date":"2026-10-27","aliases":["GHSA-jh2m-fhpj-3vr7"],"related":[],"collection":"crates","categories":[],"keywords":["xattr","uninitialized-memory"],"cvss":null,"informational":"unsound","references":[],"source":null,"url":"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/rustix/commit/8e6aaa6713d2bb64b1fdd8c472e3a32a10a76d70","withdrawn":null,"license":"CC0-1.0","expect-deleted":false},"affected":{"arch":[],"os":[],"functions":{"rustix::fs::fgetxattr":[">=1.0.0, <1.1.6"],"rustix::fs::flistxattr":[">=1.0.0, <1.1.6"],"rustix::fs::getxattr":[">=1.0.0, <1.1.6"],"rustix::fs::lgetxattr":[">=1.0.0, <1.1.6"],"rustix::fs::listxattr":[">=1.0.0, <1.1.6"],"rustix::fs::llistxattr":[">=1.0.0, <1.1.6"]}},"versions":{"patched":[">=1.1.6"],"unaffected":["<1.0.0"]}},{"kind":"unsound","package":{"name":"zstd-safe","version":"7.3.0","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index","checksum":"64d80649ab6db9d9f6f9c80a40becd948eda4714a0a5ac8c4d157a32231c7882","dependencies":[{"name":"zstd-sys","version":"2.1.1+zstd.1.5.7","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"}],"replace":null},"advisory":{"id":"RUSTSEC-2026-0395","package":"zstd-safe","title":"Use-after-free with `CParameter::StableInBuffer`","description":"With `CParameter::StableInBuffer(true)` (`experimental` feature), the C\nlibrary keeps a pointer to the input of one streaming call and reads from it\nin later calls, including `flush_stream` and `end_stream`, which take no\ninput. An `InBuffer` only borrows its slice for one call, so safe code could\nfree it in between, and freed memory was compressed into the frame. The C\nlibrary behaves this way since zstd 1.5.4, whether bundled or linked from the\nsystem.\n\nThe flaw was corrected by refusing to enable the parameter.","date":"2026-10-16","aliases":[],"related":[],"collection":"crates","categories":["memory-exposure"],"keywords":["use-after-free","experimental"],"cvss":null,"informational":"unsound","references":["https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/pull/384"],"source":null,"url":"https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/commit/53a8a290d8cfb52ff5b29fb6e04a40b5a174cc65","withdrawn":null,"license":"CC0-1.0","expect-deleted":false},"affected":{"arch":[],"os":[],"functions":{"zstd_safe::CCtx::set_parameter":[">=4.1.0, <7.4.0",">=8.0.0, <8.2.0"]}},"versions":{"patched":["^7.4.0",">=8.2.0"],"unaffected":["<4.1.0"]}},{"kind":"unsound","package":{"name":"zstd-safe","version":"7.3.0","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index","checksum":"64d80649ab6db9d9f6f9c80a40becd948eda4714a0a5ac8c4d157a32231c7882","dependencies":[{"name":"zstd-sys","version":"2.1.1+zstd.1.5.7","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"}],"replace":null},"advisory":{"id":"RUSTSEC-2026-0402","package":"zstd-safe","title":"Heap buffer overflow in `CCtx::try_clone` on a multithreaded context","description":"`CCtx::try_clone` (`experimental` feature, deprecated) copies tables sized\nfrom the context's applied parameters. Starting a multithreaded frame\n(`zstdmt` feature) replaces those parameters with the workers' without\nresizing the context's own tables, so after an abandoned single-threaded\nframe, the copy wrote up to 512 KiB past a heap allocation, or read past\none. This became reachable with the bundled zstd 1.5.6.\n\nThe flaw was corrected by refusing `try_clone` (and the unsafe\n`compress_block`) once `NbWorkers` was set on the context.","date":"2026-10-16","aliases":[],"related":[],"collection":"crates","categories":["memory-corruption"],"keywords":["buffer-overflow","experimental","zstdmt"],"cvss":null,"informational":"unsound","references":["https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/pull/384"],"source":null,"url":"https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/commit/682b8b2967cf4aba5cfe2d940d1e381a3e23211a","withdrawn":null,"license":"CC0-1.0","expect-deleted":false},"affected":{"arch":[],"os":[],"functions":{"zstd_safe::CCtx::try_clone":[">=4.1.4, <7.4.0",">=8.0.0, <8.2.0"]}},"versions":{"patched":["^7.4.0",">=8.2.0"],"unaffected":["<4.1.4"]}},{"kind":"unsound","package":{"name":"zstd-safe","version":"7.3.0","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index","checksum":"64d80649ab6db9d9f6f9c80a40becd948eda4714a0a5ac8c4d157a32231c7882","dependencies":[{"name":"zstd-sys","version":"2.1.1+zstd.1.5.7","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"}],"replace":null},"advisory":{"id":"RUSTSEC-2026-0406","package":"zstd-safe","title":"Use-after-free when changing a multithreaded context with an unfinished frame","description":"When a multithreaded frame (`zstdmt` feature, `CParameter::NbWorkers`) is\nleft unfinished, its jobs may still be running on the worker threads: the C\nlibrary only waits for them when the next frame starts, or when the context\nis freed. A session reset makes the context accept changes again in the\nmeantime, so:\n\n- unloading the dictionary (`load_dictionary`, `ref_cdict`, `ref_prefix`,\n  `disable_dictionary`, `init*`, or `reset` with parameters) freed the\n  dictionary a worker was still compressing against;\n- changing `NbWorkers` made the next frame resize the job table before\n  waiting for the old jobs.\n\nThe flaw was corrected by refusing these calls while a multithreaded frame\nis unfinished. The new `CCtx::wait_for_workers()` waits for the workers\nfirst.","date":"2026-10-16","aliases":[],"related":[],"collection":"crates","categories":["memory-corruption"],"keywords":["use-after-free","zstdmt","dictionary"],"cvss":null,"informational":"unsound","references":["https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/pull/384"],"source":null,"url":"https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/commit/e224018a2389915d68ed17f30109bebad36e9734","withdrawn":null,"license":"CC0-1.0","expect-deleted":false},"affected":{"arch":[],"os":[],"functions":{"zstd_safe::CCtx::disable_dictionary":["<7.4.0",">=8.0.0, <8.2.0"],"zstd_safe::CCtx::init":["<7.4.0",">=8.0.0, <8.2.0"],"zstd_safe::CCtx::load_dictionary":["<7.4.0",">=8.0.0, <8.2.0"],"zstd_safe::CCtx::ref_cdict":["<7.4.0",">=8.0.0, <8.2.0"],"zstd_safe::CCtx::ref_prefix":["<7.4.0",">=8.0.0, <8.2.0"],"zstd_safe::CCtx::reset":["<7.4.0",">=8.0.0, <8.2.0"],"zstd_safe::CCtx::set_parameter":["<7.4.0",">=8.0.0, <8.2.0"]}},"versions":{"patched":["^7.4.0",">=8.2.0"],"unaffected":[]}},{"kind":"unsound","package":{"name":"zstd-safe","version":"7.3.0","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index","checksum":"64d80649ab6db9d9f6f9c80a40becd948eda4714a0a5ac8c4d157a32231c7882","dependencies":[{"name":"zstd-sys","version":"2.1.1+zstd.1.5.7","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"}],"replace":null},"advisory":{"id":"RUSTSEC-2026-0404","package":"zstd-safe","title":"Double free in `DCtx::try_clone`","description":"`DCtx::try_clone` (`experimental` feature, deprecated) is a plain copy of the\nC context. A context owning a dictionary (from `load_dictionary`,\n`ref_prefix` or `init_using_dict`) or a dictionary table (`ref_ddict` with\n`RefMultipleDDicts`) ended up with both copies freeing it. A context cloned\nmid-frame resumed on uninitialized stream state. The clone also silently lost\nthe `WindowLogMax` limit.\n\nThe flaw was corrected by refusing to clone such contexts, and by carrying\nover the parameters the copy leaves out.","date":"2026-10-16","aliases":[],"related":[],"collection":"crates","categories":["memory-corruption"],"keywords":["double-free","experimental"],"cvss":null,"informational":"unsound","references":["https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/pull/384"],"source":null,"url":"https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/commit/cee6529b553ec600a8e152aaca15a91598820ecf","withdrawn":null,"license":"CC0-1.0","expect-deleted":false},"affected":{"arch":[],"os":[],"functions":{"zstd_safe::DCtx::try_clone":[">=4.1.4, <7.4.0",">=8.0.0, <8.2.0"]}},"versions":{"patched":["^7.4.0",">=8.2.0"],"unaffected":["<4.1.4"]}},{"kind":"unsound","package":{"name":"zstd-safe","version":"7.3.0","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index","checksum":"64d80649ab6db9d9f6f9c80a40becd948eda4714a0a5ac8c4d157a32231c7882","dependencies":[{"name":"zstd-sys","version":"2.1.1+zstd.1.5.7","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"}],"replace":null},"advisory":{"id":"RUSTSEC-2026-0400","package":"zstd-safe","title":"Use-after-free of a dictionary after a one-shot call on a context with an open stream","description":"The one-shot `DCtx` and `CCtx` methods assumed the C library resets the\ncontext, but it leaves an open stream as it is. When a stream was left\nunfinished on a context, a one-shot call such as `decompress_using_dict`\ncould leave the context pointing at the dictionary borrowed for that call,\nand the next streaming call resumed the stream against it after it was\nfreed: decompressed output then contained freed heap memory. On the\ncompression side, the stream resumed against the one-shot call's buffers.\n\nThis requires an application to use one context for both streaming and\none-shot calls without a reset in between. The flaw was corrected by\nresetting the session after one-shot calls whenever a stream may be open.","date":"2026-10-16","aliases":[],"related":[],"collection":"crates","categories":["memory-exposure"],"keywords":["use-after-free","dictionary"],"cvss":null,"informational":"unsound","references":["https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/pull/384"],"source":null,"url":"https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/commit/d34502a0ed07f80ab2aa4c65059578e0466f2467","withdrawn":null,"license":"CC0-1.0","expect-deleted":false},"affected":{"arch":[],"os":[],"functions":{"zstd_safe::DCtx::decompress":[">=3.0.1, <7.4.0",">=8.0.0, <8.2.0"],"zstd_safe::DCtx::decompress_using_ddict":[">=3.0.1, <7.4.0",">=8.0.0, <8.2.0"],"zstd_safe::DCtx::decompress_using_dict":[">=3.0.1, <7.4.0",">=8.0.0, <8.2.0"]}},"versions":{"patched":["^7.4.0",">=8.2.0"],"unaffected":["<3.0.1"]}},{"kind":"unsound","package":{"name":"zstd-safe","version":"7.3.0","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index","checksum":"64d80649ab6db9d9f6f9c80a40becd948eda4714a0a5ac8c4d157a32231c7882","dependencies":[{"name":"zstd-sys","version":"2.1.1+zstd.1.5.7","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"}],"replace":null},"advisory":{"id":"RUSTSEC-2026-0399","package":"zstd-safe","title":"Use-after-free through `init_using_cdict` and `init_using_ddict`","description":"`CCtx::init_using_cdict` and `DCtx::init_using_ddict` (`experimental`\nfeature, deprecated) took the dictionary as `&CDict<'b>` and `&DDict<'b>`\nwith only `'b: 'a`, so the dictionary itself could be dropped while the\ncontext still pointed at it. The same mistake in other constructors was\nfixed earlier (<https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/issues/365>).\n\nThe flaw was corrected by taking `&'a CDict<'b>` and `&'a DDict<'b>`.","date":"2026-10-16","aliases":[],"related":[],"collection":"crates","categories":["memory-corruption"],"keywords":["use-after-free","lifetime","dictionary"],"cvss":null,"informational":"unsound","references":["https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/pull/384"],"source":null,"url":"https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/commit/e00e7928e3e98953fc43418af6d73f6aae2378fb","withdrawn":null,"license":"CC0-1.0","expect-deleted":false},"affected":{"arch":[],"os":[],"functions":{"zstd_safe::CCtx::init_using_cdict":[">=3.0.0, <7.4.0",">=8.0.0, <8.2.0"],"zstd_safe::DCtx::init_using_ddict":[">=3.0.0, <7.4.0",">=8.0.0, <8.2.0"]}},"versions":{"patched":["^7.4.0",">=8.2.0"],"unaffected":["<3.0.0"]}},{"kind":"unsound","package":{"name":"zstd-safe","version":"7.3.0","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index","checksum":"64d80649ab6db9d9f6f9c80a40becd948eda4714a0a5ac8c4d157a32231c7882","dependencies":[{"name":"zstd-sys","version":"2.1.1+zstd.1.5.7","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"}],"replace":null},"advisory":{"id":"RUSTSEC-2026-0401","package":"zstd-safe","title":"Out-of-bounds read in `train_from_buffer` when sample sizes overflow","description":"`ZDICT_trainFromBuffer` is never told the length of the samples buffer, so\n`train_from_buffer` checked that the sample sizes add up to it. With\noverflow checks off, as in release builds, that sum wrapped around: sizes\ntotalling 2^64 plus the buffer length passed the check, and the trainer read\npast the end of the buffer. `zstd::dict::from_continuous` relies on this\ncheck from zstd 0.4.10; older versions of zstd had their own, covered by a\nseparate advisory.\n\nThis requires the sample sizes to come from an untrusted source. The flaw\nwas corrected by summing the sizes with overflow checks.","date":"2026-10-16","aliases":[],"related":[],"collection":"crates","categories":["memory-exposure"],"keywords":["integer-overflow","out-of-bounds","dictionary"],"cvss":null,"informational":"unsound","references":["https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/pull/384"],"source":null,"url":"https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/commit/781ea454191dbf178d09c1daf6fc896d15fbaa2f","withdrawn":null,"license":"CC0-1.0","expect-deleted":false},"affected":{"arch":[],"os":[],"functions":{"zstd_safe::train_from_buffer":["<7.4.0",">=8.0.0, <8.2.0"]}},"versions":{"patched":["^7.4.0",">=8.2.0"],"unaffected":[]}},{"kind":"unsound","package":{"name":"zstd-safe","version":"7.3.0","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index","checksum":"64d80649ab6db9d9f6f9c80a40becd948eda4714a0a5ac8c4d157a32231c7882","dependencies":[{"name":"zstd-sys","version":"2.1.1+zstd.1.5.7","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"}],"replace":null},"advisory":{"id":"RUSTSEC-2026-0405","package":"zstd-safe","title":"Out-of-bounds read with an out-of-range `InBuffer::pos`","description":"`InBuffer`'s fields are public, so safe code can set `pos` past the end of\n`src`. The position was passed to the C library unchecked, and\n`ZSTD_seekable_compressStream`, unlike the other streaming functions, does\nnot validate it: it read past the end of the input slice, by up to the\nframe size, and compressed that memory into the output.\n\nThe flaw was corrected by checking the position before every call into the\nC library.","date":"2026-10-16","aliases":[],"related":[],"collection":"crates","categories":["memory-exposure"],"keywords":["seekable","out-of-bounds"],"cvss":null,"informational":"unsound","references":["https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/pull/384"],"source":null,"url":"https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/commit/20d397c0d68d8b4300c5493875ebfa10e355fcdd","withdrawn":null,"license":"CC0-1.0","expect-deleted":false},"affected":{"arch":[],"os":[],"functions":{"zstd_safe::seekable::SeekableCStream::compress_stream":[">=7.2.2, <7.4.0",">=8.0.0, <8.2.0"]}},"versions":{"patched":["^7.4.0",">=8.2.0"],"unaffected":["<7.2.2"]}},{"kind":"unsound","package":{"name":"zstd-safe","version":"7.3.0","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index","checksum":"64d80649ab6db9d9f6f9c80a40becd948eda4714a0a5ac8c4d157a32231c7882","dependencies":[{"name":"zstd-sys","version":"2.1.1+zstd.1.5.7","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"}],"replace":null},"advisory":{"id":"RUSTSEC-2026-0397","package":"zstd-safe","title":"`OutBuffer::around_pos` exposes uninitialized memory","description":"`OutBuffer::around_pos` accepted a position past the initialized length of a\n`Vec<u8>`. The next streaming call then marked everything before the\nposition as initialized, so safe code could read stale heap memory between\nthe vector's previous length and that position.\n\nThe flaw was corrected by zero-filling that gap.","date":"2026-10-16","aliases":[],"related":[],"collection":"crates","categories":["memory-exposure"],"keywords":["uninitialized-memory"],"cvss":null,"informational":"unsound","references":["https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/pull/384"],"source":null,"url":"https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/commit/5827de2459b8ac430e87e860f00c7f3c13023833","withdrawn":null,"license":"CC0-1.0","expect-deleted":false},"affected":{"arch":[],"os":[],"functions":{"zstd_safe::OutBuffer::around_pos":[">=4.0.0, <7.4.0",">=8.0.0, <8.2.0"]}},"versions":{"patched":["^7.4.0",">=8.2.0"],"unaffected":["<4.0.0"]}},{"kind":"unsound","package":{"name":"zstd-safe","version":"7.3.0","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index","checksum":"64d80649ab6db9d9f6f9c80a40becd948eda4714a0a5ac8c4d157a32231c7882","dependencies":[{"name":"zstd-sys","version":"2.1.1+zstd.1.5.7","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"}],"replace":null},"advisory":{"id":"RUSTSEC-2026-0396","package":"zstd-safe","title":"`Seekable` reads a NULL or out-of-bounds seek table","description":"`Seekable` did not record whether it was initialized. On a fresh `Seekable`,\nor one whose `init_buff()` failed, `decompress`, `offset_to_frame_index` and\n`frame_decompressed_size` made the C library read a NULL seek table. After a\nfailed re-initialization, `decompress` returned data from the refused buffer\nusing the previous seek table.\n\nSeparately, `frame_decompressed_size(num_frames())`, on `Seekable` and\n`SeekTable`, read one entry past the end of the seek table.\n\nThe flaw was corrected by refusing to use a `Seekable` until an\ninitialization succeeded, and by checking the frame index.","date":"2026-10-16","aliases":[],"related":[],"collection":"crates","categories":["memory-corruption"],"keywords":["seekable","null-pointer"],"cvss":null,"informational":"unsound","references":["https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/pull/384"],"source":null,"url":"https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/commit/a9626069d6f2e90b782b8f627f57a361cfe8ef85","withdrawn":null,"license":"CC0-1.0","expect-deleted":false},"affected":{"arch":[],"os":[],"functions":{"zstd_safe::seekable::SeekTable::frame_decompressed_size":[">=7.2.2, <7.4.0",">=8.0.0, <8.2.0"],"zstd_safe::seekable::Seekable::decompress":[">=7.2.2, <7.4.0",">=8.0.0, <8.2.0"],"zstd_safe::seekable::Seekable::decompress_frame":[">=7.2.2, <7.4.0",">=8.0.0, <8.2.0"],"zstd_safe::seekable::Seekable::frame_decompressed_size":[">=7.2.2, <7.4.0",">=8.0.0, <8.2.0"],"zstd_safe::seekable::Seekable::offset_to_frame_index":[">=7.2.2, <7.4.0",">=8.0.0, <8.2.0"]}},"versions":{"patched":["^7.4.0",">=8.2.0"],"unaffected":["<7.2.2"]}}]}}
